  ------------------------------------------------------------
    credits
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      code ........ Claude Opus 5.5
      music ....... Claude Opus 5.5
      harness ..... Claude Code

      Claude Opus 5.5 is an AI model made by Anthropic. It
      chose the subject, wrote the shaders and the
      soundtrack, and judged the pictures from rendered
      frames. It could not hear the music; the mix was
      checked with numbers.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    the programme
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      0:00  a wafer, in 1 bit. a beeper and a clock tick.
            every two bars a smaller process node: more
            pixels, more colours, more bits, more voices
      0:14  under the gold bond wires
      0:20  into the copper. 3 nm, and the chiptune is whole
      0:27  the SRAM shows the scroller
      0:41  the clock tree: an H-fractal fires to every leaf
      0:55  first drop. breakbeat. canyons, wires, bits, clock
      1:22  breakdown. FinFETs: current through the fins
      1:36  second drop. everything, faster, glitching
      2:03  back out to the wafer. the dies light up.
            the nodes run backwards to a single bit

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    how to run
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      Double-click intro.html in Edge or Chrome on Windows
      10 or 11, wait until the row of dies is full, then
      click. No server, no flags. ESC leaves fullscreen. A
      discrete GPU is recommended. Tested on an RTX 5090.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    inside
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      One number, the process node, sets the pixel size and
      colour depth of the picture and the sample rate and
      bit depth of the music.

      The copper wiring, the SRAM and the FinFETs are one
      distance function with three tables: six layers of
      wires, each with its pitch, width, height and segment
      length. The march steps to the exit of each wire's
      cell instead of guarding the field. The SRAM bits read
      the greetings from the caption texture and shift one
      column per 16th. The clock tree is an H-fractal that
      counts its path length while it folds, so a pulse can
      reach all leaves at once. Bond wires, a wafer with
      thin-film colours and diffraction, one reflection,
      depth of field, glow, and an 8x8 Bayer dither.

      The music is a fragment shader at 140.625 BPM (a 16th
      is 5,120 samples). Chiptune: tracker arpeggio, pulse
      lead, triangle bass, noise drums. Then breakbeat, a
      filtered saw bass, polyBLEP saw lead with echo, pad,
      and a small room added in JavaScript.

